<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Isenburg-Büdingen</h1><p class="st-meta">1517 CE – 1806 CE (approx.) · capital Büdingen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Wetterau county of the house of Isenburg, centred on Büdingen castle, that sheltered religious dissenters in the 18th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The house of Isenburg held lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wetterau">Wetterau</a> and around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budingen">Büdingen</a> from the 13th century. A division of the family's possessions in 1517 created the Büdingen line alongside the Birstein branch, and further partitions followed in the 17th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts ruled a hilly, wooded territory from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budingen">Büdingen's</a> walled town and castle. They adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a>. Witch trials in the 16th and 17th centuries were severe. In 1712 Count Ernst Casimir issued an edict of tolerance that drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/community-of-true-inspiration">Inspirationists</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-church">Moravian Brethren</a> and other dissenters to the Ronneburg and Marienborn.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1806 the county was mediatised and placed under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-isenburg">Principality of Isenburg</a>, and in 1816 it passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse</a>. The princes of Ysenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budingen">Büdingen</a> still own Büdingen castle.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-isenburg-budingen.jpg" alt="County of Isenburg-Büdingen" width="320" height="286"><figcaption>Image: Glasshouse · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_the_Principality_of_Isenburg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1517 CE – 1806 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Büdingen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ernst Casimir I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ysenburg-Büdingen, Isenburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1662&amp;focus=county-of-isenburg-budingen" aria-label="County of Isenburg-Büdingen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4888 430 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Isenburg-Büdingen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5208 686L5229 694M5208 686L5173 680"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5173 680h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5208 686h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5208 686h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Büdingen. <a href="/atlas?year=1662&amp;focus=county-of-isenburg-budingen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
